/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  kinetics01_oh_decay -- the cell of a flash-photolysis experiment, run by the
  engine (choupoBatch).  The witness of the EduTool `how-do-we-know-k`.

  WHAT IT IS.  An isothermal closed vessel at 1000 K and 1 bar holds H2 in N2
  (mole fraction 0.01) and a trace of OH (1e-6) made at t = 0: the radical a
  flash would leave.  The whole O Conaire et al. 2004 H2/O2 mechanism (Int. J.
  Chem. Kinet. 36, 603, Table I; the same constant/reactions as
  ignition01_h2o2) is switched on, so the OH can do everything the mechanism
  lets it do.  With the H2 in a large excess the OH decays by OH + H2 = H2O + H
  alone, as a first-order law: ln n_OH falls on a straight line of slope -k',
  k' = k c_H2, the pseudo-first-order constant of the experimenter's method.
  The window (100 us) holds about thirteen lifetimes at these conditions.

  WHAT THE PAGE DOES WITH IT.  The EduTool rewrites T, P, the charge, the H2,
  OH and H2O2 mole fractions and the window (declared numbers of 0/ and of this
  file), runs the cell, fits the decay and compares the k it recovers with the
  law the engine announced for the reaction when it loaded it.  The same case
  seeded with H2O2 instead of OH is the fall-off experiment (R15).
  `rateOfProduction` (flowsheetDict) prints which reactions make and destroy
  each species at 5 us: the secondary chemistry that contaminates a measured
  decay when the OH seed is large.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

application   choupoBatch;
description   "Flash-photolysis cell: a trace of OH decaying in H2/N2 at 1000 K, 1 bar, isothermal (O Conaire 2004 H2/O2 kinetics); the signal the EduTool how-do-we-know-k fits for k";

verbosity     3;

// ---- Time integration ----
startTime       0;          // s
endTime         1.0e-4;     // s  (about thirteen OH lifetimes at the defaults)
deltaT          5.0e-7;     // s  (the stiff integrator sub-steps adaptively within each deltaT)
writeInterval   5.0e-7;     // s  (200 instants across the window)
